{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-160481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2b769e96211c5cfefcda09189202987bc1f7cbf1c7a7a942c39344d75f3d966d"} {"question" "q-160481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ad0e3ddbeb228e3fdeeb09823493fb54a4a7f930c25ecd3b69b71dcf77e1d923.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{7},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/2fbee04fdedee3c2ef0b0f0da2022909ff8d9c2bea7e16b158517afe8fc35c43.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2b769e96211c5cfefcda09189202987bc1f7cbf1c7a7a942c39344d75f3d966d"} {"question" "q-160910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/2fbee04fdedee3c2ef0b0f0da2022909ff8d9c2bea7e16b158517afe8fc35c43.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/5bb9d3386eab3293e8319d7604b13e4a530f8311610ba8f0aba8f70e11aa32b0.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/a31fe0c820f7d69c3a6c7bb64c3deac3b0be917f1edea273feac2353edb2df58.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/d951dd353ee85920ecaf73724defb0f8a9f902810f3ce333da54fee96ca2eba1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/f8e51d08054d568fc7caad893e7c82421e032c45b962399fd02d5c90bbe83e7f.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/2fbee04fdedee3c2ef0b0f0da2022909ff8d9c2bea7e16b158517afe8fc35c43.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/85e8422a0b3421db73d8cbe88ef3c51b59f628f233d48982840a21c4d26bd26f.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2b769e96211c5cfefcda09189202987bc1f7cbf1c7a7a942c39344d75f3d966d"} {"question" "q-160928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/10a9d70717e657082fd8c36dc98876fcea53297ce79b44585574efef821dba51.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/90571cebb5f46697204869a7dbd07e83036d4fe92a5f46045bf6279b3b307999.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/9d781ed8813991d36d3da4802efa94543f1ee3fc542e1b8c72ff7ad521974e58.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/e718cdc16345d0da40874901ed4dc276c5cf33a75949365b59129a7bb934a8c1.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-160928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/16d74107dbbf12749766cd9cea7e38d16e28a59efb997bd224aed73c7830324e.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{10\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2b769e96211c5cfefcda09189202987bc1f7cbf1c7a7a942c39344d75f3d966d"} {"question" "q-161032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/aa8020019d441b741a9b621a0015fda31b3aa199afd8fc145aa840b1749e7a8b.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ab94778cea0a627b227e39745bc9efe3f516b5bcfb5560ee70d6fd30fa58289a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2b769e96211c5cfefcda09189202987bc1f7cbf1c7a7a942c39344d75f3d966d"} {"question" "q-161108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/2804981f7e56212c632bab38ff49f7b1a641f708b69dbaa1228cccd82c9865c7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/41c26aac58a4c8c2b577f9d3d17c90bd6cbd6971709c42f8a74117db180da899.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/4a10a6686caaf68c7986ce9c4b60f78f1a21c6414b02133e6c9c82461e1ad33b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/72c832e8bd3990885826013f8ab49e5e50bd6ac3bb567b999e0a8a2bbdf5f346.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ab94778cea0a627b227e39745bc9efe3f516b5bcfb5560ee70d6fd30fa58289a.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"} {"question" "q-161108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ab94778cea0a627b227e39745bc9efe3f516b5bcfb5560ee70d6fd30fa58289a.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca80e80e6f6f9b00b3662815a9aeb88d65c5b28aa6cc903a63e850b42a15a3a"}] "usage" {"q-160481" []} "basis" 2594 "retractions" [[17592186198507 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160481.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 1 \\le u \\le \\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{6} \\le v \\le \\frac{π}{3}}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (u\\cos v, u\\sin v)$.\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n1. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{π}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n2. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{π}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n3. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186193944 17592186193945 17592186193946 17592186193947 17592186193948] "immutable_fields" [[17592186193943 :answer-field/correct] [17592186193943 :answer-field/id] [17592186193943 :answer-field/key] [17592186193943 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
